$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Procedure for Reductive Aminations. Benzaldehyde (0.4 mL, 3.9
mmol) and propan-1-amine (0.48 mL, 5.9 mmol) were dissolved in 3.3 mL of
THF (1.2 M), each in separate vials. A sample of 1.5 mL of methanol (37.2
mmol) was divided between the two vials to create equal volumes. After
flushing the column with THF, the two reagents were combined, using the
Vapourtec software, each at a flow rate of 0.2 mL/min. The entirety of
the output was collected (including one column volume of pure solvent
following the reaction to ensure full elution of product), washed with
distilled water (20 mL), and washed three times with dichloromethane (15
mL × 3). The combined organic phase was dried over Na2SO4, filtered, and
dried using rotary evaporation. N-Benzylpropanamine. N-Benzylpropanamine
was prepared according to general procedure for reductive amination with
benzaldehyde (0.416 g) dissolved in a mixture of MeOH (0.8 mL) and THF
(3.3 mL), and propylamine (0.348 g) was dissolved in a mixture of MeOH
(0.7 mL) and THF (3.3 mL). After silica gel column chromatography (1:5
ethyl acetate−n-hexane), pure N-propylbenzyl amine (0.468 g) was obtained
as a colorless oil (80%). Phenylmethanol. N-Benzylpropanamine: 1H NMR:
7.33 (m, 5H), 3.80 (s, 2H), 2.61 (t, J = 8 Hz, 2H), 1.94 (br s, 1H), 1.54
(sextet, J = 8 Hz, 2H), 0.92 (t, J = 8 Hz, 3H). Phenylmethanol: 1H NMR:
7.38−7.29 (m, 5H), 4.67 (s, 2H), 2.1 (s, 1H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM NaBH4/Celite/LiCl column used, selective, gram scale
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Continuous Reductions and Reductive Aminations Using Solid NaBH4
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Gilmore, Kerry; Vukelic, Stella; McQuade, D. Tyler; Koksch, Beate;
Seeberger, Peter H.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Process Research & Development (2014), 18(12), 1771-1776

