$DATUM 67-56-1
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Compound 45, [Phe2,Ile3,Hgn4,Orn(iPr)8]VP The following amino acid
derivatives were used: Boc-Cys(Trt)-OH, Fmoc-Phe-OH, Fmoc- Ile-OH,
Fmoc-Hgn-OH, Fmoc-Asn(Trt)-OH, Fmoc-Cys(Trt)-OH, Fmoc-Pro-OH,
Fmoc-Orn(Mmt)-OH and Fmoc-Gly-OH. Fmoc-Hgn-OH was synthesised according
to reference 48 (see the paper). Analytical HPLC was performed on a
Waters 600 Liquid Chromatograph using a Vydac C18, 5μm, 4.6 x 250 mm
column at a flow rate of 2 mL/min. Preparative HPLC was performed on a
Waters 2000 Liquid Chromatograph using a Prepak 47 x 300 mm cartridge at
a flow rate of 100 mL/min. HPLC analytical methods to determine final
purity of analogues, retention times (tR) and HPLC capacity factors (k')
are described in the Experimental section of the paper. Mass spectra were
recorded on a Finnigan MAT spectrometer. The fully protected peptide
resin was synthesized on a continuous-flow Applied Biosystems 9050
Peptide Synthesizer starting from 2 g (0.5 mmol) of Tentagel-S-RAM resin
(Peptides International). DIC/HOBt mediated single couplings with a
4-fold excess of amino acid derivatives were employed. The Fmoc group was
removed with 20% piperidine in DMF. Upon completion of the automated
synthesis, the resin was transferred into a manual synthesis vessel and
was treated with DCM/TIS/TFA 93/5/2 (v/v/v) solution (30 mL) for 2 x 1.5
hours for removal of the Mmt group. The resin was thoroughly washed with
DCM and was subsequently suspended in 15 mL of 1,2-dichloroetehane/TMOF
1:1 (v/v). 0.2 mL of acetone was then added followed by 0.6 g of
NaBH(OAc)3. The suspension was shaken overnight and the resin was washed
with methanol, DMF and DCM and dried in vacuo. The resin was then treated
with 30 mL of the TFA/TIS/H2O 96/2/2 (v/v/v) solution for 1.5 hours and
filtered off. The filtrate was evaporated and the crude linear peptide
was precipitated with diethyl ether. The precipitate was immediately
dissolved in 500 mL of 10% TFA (aq), and the peptide was oxidized by
adding 0.1 M I2 in methanol to the magnetically stirred solution until
yellow color persisted. Excess of iodine was reduced with solid ascorbic
acid. The reaction mixture was then cooled with crushed ice and pH was
adjusted to about 5 by adding concentrated ammonia (aq). The mixture was
loaded onto an HPLC column and purified using a triethylammonium
phosphate buffer with pH 5.2. The compound was eluted with a gradient of
acetonitrile. The fractions with a purity exceeding 97% were pooled, and
the resulting solution was diluted with 2 volumes of water. The solution
was reloaded onto the column which was then washed with 2 l of 0.1 M
ammonium acetate (aq) and equilibrated with 2% acetic acid (aq). The
compound was eluted with a fast (3%/min) gradient of acetonitrile. The
fractions containing the desired product were pooled and lyophilized. 168
mg (~30% yield) of white amorphous powder was obtained Compound 45, yield
30%. HPLC: Rt = 8.5 min, gradient: 20→ B over 20 min, t=40°C, solvent A
0.01% TFA (aq), solvent B 70% CH3CN, 0.01% TFA (aq); Purity: 98.8%; MS
(M+H+): expected 1048.5, observed 1048.5.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM solid-supported reaction, peptide synthesizer used, Fmoc deprotection
with piperidine/DMF, attachment to Tentagel-S-RAM resin in stage 1,
process repeated twice in stage 10
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM New, potent, selective, and short-acting peptidic V1a receptor agonists
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Wisniewski, Kazimierz; Galyean, Robert; Tariga, Hiroe; Alagarsamy,
Sudarkodi; Croston, Glenn; Heitzmann, Joshua; Kohan, Arash; Wisniewska,
Halina; Laporte, Regent; Riviere, Pierre J-M.; Schteingart, Claudio D.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of Medicinal Chemistry (2011), 54(13), 4388-4398

$RFMT $RIREG SCHEME231 STEP1
